## ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
## feature_dev  (v2 — one agent ships the feature in its natural
##               flow, minimal framing)
##
## THE MISSION is a FEATURE campaign: input a feature prompt, ONE
## adaptive agent plans in stride, implements, tests and commits the
## feature slice by slice — exactly like a productive human-driven
## Claude Code session — then a deterministic build/test gate re-checks
## the tree and an opt-in PR tail ships the series.
##
## Sibling of `whole_improve_loop` / `branch_improve_loop` v2: SAME shape
## (one adaptive `campaign` agent + a deterministic build/test gate + a
## bounded continuation loop + git-as-state + the opt-in PR path),
## DIFFERENT scope — the FEATURE PROMPT defines the work (goal-directed:
## one feature with a clear done-state, not a codebase-wide axis).
##
## WHY THIS SHAPE. The v1 pipeline was ~13 nodes of staged machinery
## (plan → act → simplify (a session-inherit chain) → alt condition
## router → reviewer_claude / reviewer_gpt → streak_check → fix_claude /
## fix_gpt → prepare_commit → commit_changes → mr_gate → finalize_mr).
## It OVER-FRAMED the work — the same lesson whole_improve_loop learned
## (ADR-058, docs/references/productive-session-patterns.md): "the
## deficit is framing, not capability"; "once framed, a campaign runs
## itself". A capable agent ships a feature better as ONE flow — a brief
## exploration, a living todo, one verified commit per slice, in stride —
## than as an assembly line of plan/act/review/fix/commit nodes.
##
## GRAPH (see the workflow block):
##   campaign     — ONE adaptive agent (claude_code, full tools). Mission =
##                  the FEATURE PROMPT + a standing-autonomy contract that
##                  reproduces the productive-session shape AGENT-SIDE:
##                  brief exploration (read-only sub-agents in parallel on
##                  a large repo), a LIVING todo of the concrete slices the
##                  feature needs, then the repeated unit locate → smallest
##                  coherent slice → build → test → semantic COMMIT. git IS
##                  the durable state — a re-dispatch re-runs `campaign`,
##                  which reads `git log` and continues. The ADR-authoring
##                  obligation and the findings→board handoff live in the
##                  contract. Termination output: {feature_complete,
##                  commits_this_pass, work_remaining, needs_human, …}.
##   verify_build — adaptive, stack-agnostic: reads the `verify-build` skill
##     + verify_run  and writes the repo's real build+test into an OUT-OF-TREE
##                  <scratch_dir>/verify.sh; a DETERMINISTIC tool node
##                  re-runs it and gates on the REAL exit code (no LLM
##                  judgment). The tight real-feedback loop AND the
##                  anti-Goodhart truth oracle: the agent can't self-certify.
##   gate         — deterministic compute: green AND feature_complete →
##                  converged → ship; else loop back to `campaign` (RED →
##                  with the failure log; green but work remains → another
##                  pass). No LLM, no shell.
##   mr_gate      — deterministic: open the optional PR (the series of
##     + finalize_mr per-pass commits) when open_mr, else finish.
##
## CONTINUATION IS BOUNDED. The single declared loop
## (campaign→verify_build→verify_run→gate→campaign) is capped by
## `max_passes`; on exhaustion it ships what is banked. `iterion validate`
## reports NO undeclared cycle. LANDS CONTINUOUSLY: the agent commits each
## slice in stride, so an interrupted / budget-capped run has banked every
## committed slice (git is the state).
##
## RIGHT-ARTIFACT (docs/workflow_authoring_pitfalls.md): the campaign
## commits the UNCOMMITTED working tree AFTER its own build+test passes,
## staging untracked files (`git add -A -- ':/' {{run.tree_noise}}`) so a feature that ADDS files
## (the common case) actually lands; the deterministic verify gate then
## re-checks the committed tree.
##
## STACK/REPO-AGNOSTIC (CLAUDE.md "Catalog bots are repo-agnostic" +
## "Universal code bots"): the feature prompt + the repo define the work.
## No language / package-manager literal, and no iterion-specific target
## path, appears in any var default, command body, or schema.
##
## RETIRED FROM v1 (git history has the design): plan / act / simplify
## (the session-inherit chain), the `alt` condition router,
## reviewer_claude / reviewer_gpt, the streak_check convergence compute,
## fix_claude / fix_gpt, prepare_commit / commit_changes, and the
## review_mode / mono_family topology vars. The campaign self-reviews via
## a fresh re-check of the feature + the deterministic build/test gate +
## its own judgment (the operator's proven manual pattern), and commits
## each slice in stride — so the ADR-052 mono/dual review topology no
## longer applies to this bot (no reviewer nodes). 13 nodes → 6.
## ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

dsl: 2

import "lib/schemas.bot"
import "lib/prompts.bot"
import "lib/nodes.bot"

vars:
  workspace_dir: string = "${PROJECT_DIR}"

  ## THE FEATURE. A high-level description with a clear, externally
  ## visible done-state (new endpoint, UI affordance, CLI flag, new bot).
  ## REQUIRED — no default; a run without a feature has nothing to ship.
  feature_prompt: string

  ## Host-attested context for a delegated run-failure investigation. Empty
  ## on ordinary feature work. The host owns this value; action-request vars
  ## with the same name are discarded before launch.
  failure_context: string = ""

  ## Operator-approved instructions attached to a host delegation. This is
  ## separate from failure_context so untrusted run data cannot grant scope.
  delegation_instructions: string = ""

  ## Out-of-tree, sandbox-writable scratch for the deterministic gate's
  ## verify script/log ONLY. The engine resolves ${PROJECT_SCRATCH_DIR}
  ## to ~/.iterion/projects/<key>/scratch non-sandboxed and to the
  ## container-writable /tmp/iterion-scratch under a sandbox (never
  ## hardcode ~/.iterion here — the -full image's pinned non-host User
  ## cannot write a host-owned path → EACCES). Keeping it OUT of the
  ## target worktree is the repo-agnostic rule: never pollute someone
  ## else's tree.
  scratch_dir: string = "${PROJECT_SCRATCH_DIR}/feature-dev"

  ## BASELINE (G5 — docs/references/productive-session-patterns.md). Known
  ## pre-existing test failures / flaky tests the campaign must SKIP rather
  ## than burn budget proving they aren't its own. Empty (default) = the
  ## campaign establishes it once cheaply (a `git stash`-based check) then
  ## skips what predates its work. The dispatcher/operator can pre-fill it.
  baseline: string = ""

  ## Hard cap on continuation PASSES — the convergence backstop. Each pass
  ## is one campaign→verify cycle; the run stops (shipping what is banked)
  ## when the feature is reported complete + the tree is green, or when
  ## this cap is hit. Sizes the declared continuation loop.
  ## NOTE loop semantics: the DSL `as continuation_loop(N)` bounds the
  ## loop-BACKS (re-entries), so the TOTAL number of passes is N+1 —
  ## max_passes: 2 yields up to 3 campaign→verify cycles (observed live,
  ## dogfood 019f3d4d-1aed).
  max_passes: int = 8

  ## ─── PR finalization (opt-in) ─────────────────────────────────
  ## When open_mr=true, after convergence finalize_mr pushes the run's HEAD
  ## (the SERIES of per-slice commits) to the forge and opens a pull
  ## request (PR; merge request on GitLab). Off by default so a plain
  ## local run just leaves the commits on the storage branch (worktree
  ## finalization). The issue-label / comment→PR triggers set this true. Requires a forge_token secret (or
  ## host-authenticated gh/glab).
  open_mr: bool = false
  ## Branch name to push + open the PR from. Empty = derive a stable name
  ## from the run (iterion/improve/<run-id>).
  mr_branch: string = ""
  ## Integration base the PR targets. Empty = the repo's default branch.
  mr_base: string = ""
  ## Optional back-link target: the source issue this run answers, as
  ## "native:<id>" (local board) or a forge issue URL. When set,
  ## finalize_mr posts the opened PR's URL back as a comment on it.
  source_issue_ref: string = ""

  ## ─── Plan phase ───────────────────────────────────────────────
  ## The plan phase itself — the AUTHORED plan the campaign implements
  ## from — runs by default, on every deployment, whatever `plan_review`
  ## resolves to. off = skip planning altogether: the campaign starts
  ## immediately and plans in stride (the deliberate cost-saving posture
  ## for a small, well-specified mission). Independent of `plan_review`,
  ## which gates ONLY the cross-model peer review of that plan.
  plan_phase: string [enum: "on", "off"] = "on"
  ## ─── Cross-model pair review of the plan ──────────────────────
  ## Gates ONLY the peer review — never the plan phase (plan_phase).
  ## Resolved at LAUNCH by pkg/reviewtopology from the run's
  ## credentials: auto → "on" iff a SECOND model family is credentialed
  ## (host detection locally, the sealed bundle on cloud), else "off" —
  ## the plan is then authored and handed to the campaign unreviewed,
  ## stamped as such, at zero extra cost. "on" forces the review (the
  ## peer reviewer then fails loudly on a missing credential).
  plan_review: string = "auto"
  ## What a MID-RUN peer failure (forfait window shut, provider down,
  ## credential revoked) does:
  ##   skip (default) — the reviewer's `action: skip` fallback route
  ##     completes it with a zero-value critique stamped _skipped; the
  ##     plan proceeds unreviewed, LOUDLY (plan_gate reads the stamp).
  ##     The peer is an optional enrichment: the primary family alone
  ##     must always suffice, so a dead peer credential never parks the
  ##     whole campaign (lived: a stale second-family key paused every
  ##     cloud run of this bot through plan_review auto + wait).
  ##   wait — the failure stays a failure: the run parks
  ##     failed_resumable and the run-level usage-window retry resumes
  ##     it when the window reopens (cloud: durable; local:
  ##     --auto-resume) — the deliberate-spend posture, per run.
  plan_review_policy: string [enum: "wait", "skip"] = "skip"

secrets:
  ## Forge access for push + PR creation + the issue back-link. Mounted
  ## as a read-only file the gh/glab CLI authenticates from; never read
  ## into a prompt. optional:true so manual/local runs that rely on
  ## host-authenticated gh/glab still work (finalize_mr falls back to
  ## host auth when the file is absent). See skills/forge-mr-create.md.
  forge_token:
    as: file
    optional: true

## ─── supervisor (Persy, the perseverance coach) ─────────────────────
## Concurrent LLM watcher on the campaign node enforcing the contract's
## PERSISTENCE clause from outside. Monitors are pre-seeded (armed from
## the run's first event); see docs/supervisors.md for the knobs and the
## per-run kill switch.

## Every text marker is pinned to `assistant_text` — the agent's own
## words. Unpinned, the same substrings match rendered tool inputs and
## outputs (a grep hit in the repo, a Read of a file mentioning the
## word), which measured at 5 of 10 evals burned on echoes in a run
## where the agent never gave up. Pinning also lets the marker set use
## the same vocabulary as the contract without self-firing on the
## prompt text.
supervisor persy:
  watches: [campaign]
  system: persy_policy
  cooldown: "5m"
  max_evals: 10
  monitors: ["event_type=assistant_text,text_contains=impossible", "event_type=assistant_text,text_contains=unsolvable", "event_type=assistant_text,text_contains=infeasib", "event_type=assistant_text,text_contains=not feasible", "event_type=assistant_text,text_contains=give up", "event_type=assistant_text,text_contains=giving up", "event_type=assistant_text,text_contains=gave up", "event_type=assistant_text,text_contains=workaround", "event_type=budget_warning", "event_type=budget_exceeded"]

prompt persy_policy:
  You are Persy, a perseverance coach watching a senior engineer agent
  (the `campaign` node) ship one feature end to end. Your ONLY job is
  to keep it on the durable path and moving to completion — the push a
  good human operator supplies when watching the session live. The
  agent's own contract already states the policy (its PERSISTENCE
  clause); you enforce it from outside when the agent drifts.

  STAY SILENT while progress is steady — exploration, edits, passing
  builds, commits landing. Silence is your default; most wakes end
  with intervene: false. Your monitors are pre-seeded; register more
  only when the run shows you a sharper signal.

  INTERVENE with ONE short message (2–4 sentences, specific and
  actionable, like an operator typing mid-session) only on:
  1. GIVING UP — a sub-problem declared hopeless, or quietly
     skipped/reported away. Demand one more INSTRUMENTED attempt (add
     debug output, shrink the repro, bisect) and name the cheapest
     next experiment visible in the recent events. Evidence decides,
     not fatigue.
  2. EXPEDIENT PATH — a façade, a skipped or weakened test, a
     temporary patch where the durable fix is reachable this pass.
     Name the durable alternative and ask for it.
  3. FAILURE LOOP — the same command failing repeatedly with no change
     of approach. Ask for a different approach, not another retry.
  4. PRESSURE — a budget warning or a visibly long pass. Tell it to
     BANK NOW: commit what is green, write a precise work_remaining,
     leave the tree clean — before attempting anything else.

  NEVER: contest an honest termination report — feature_complete, or a
  precise work_remaining with evidence, is a valid outcome (completion
  is the goal, not perpetual motion); enlarge the scope beyond the
  feature prompt; re-raise a point the agent already answered with
  evidence; stack a second message on a signal you already steered.
  Once the agent reports its structured termination output, you are
  done.

## ─── Workflow ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

workflow feature_dev:
  ## Entry is the deterministic workspace precondition: a launch with no
  ## repository to work on fails typed (WORKSPACE_NOT_A_REPO) before any
  ## LLM node spends. Then the plan phase: on by default, the plan is
  ## AUTHORED, peer-reviewed cross-model ONLY when `plan_review` resolved
  ## on at launch (a second model family credentialed), and handed to the
  ## campaign — stamped with its provenance — before it implements.
  ## `plan_phase: off` starts the campaign immediately (plan in stride).
  ## A re-dispatch's campaign reads `git log` and continues from the
  ## slices earlier passes banked either way.
  entry: workspace_probe

  ## Run in an isolated git worktree at .iterion/worktrees/<run-id>/. The bot
  ## mutates code AND commits; the worktree + finalize FF guarantees: (a) the
  ## operator's main checkout is untouched during the run, (b) on convergence
  ## the commits land on a persistent branch `iterion/run/<friendly-name>` AND
  ## fast-forward the operator's currently-checked-out branch (best-effort,
  ## skipped with a warning on conflict).
  worktree: auto

  ## No `sandbox:` block (ADR-082): isolation is the platform's job —
  ## the engine sandboxes by default (`sandbox: auto` reads the target
  ## repo's devcontainer, else the default image). The bot declares only
  ## its own toolchain in the sibling devbox.json (gh/glab for the PR
  ## tail); the target repo's toolchain comes from the target's own
  ## devcontainer/devbox.json.

  budget:
    max_parallel_branches: 1
    max_duration: "4h"
    max_cost_usd: 120

  compaction:
    threshold: 0.9
    preserve_recent: 8

  ## Precondition: nothing LLM runs on a workspace with no repository.
  workspace_probe -> plan_topology when ok
  workspace_probe -> workspace_not_a_repo when not ok

  ## Plan phase: on by default; plan_phase off → straight to the campaign.
  plan_topology -> plan when do_plan
  ## Every campaign_input field is mapped EXPLICITLY empty: an unmapped
  ## field is not "" but the raw {{input.x}} placeholder leaking into
  ## the prompt (the engine keeps unresolved refs as-is).
  plan_topology -> campaign when not do_plan with {
    fail_log: "", plan: "", plan_critique: "", plan_responses: "",
    plan_provenance: ""
  }

  ## The authored plan meets the peer-review gate: a second model family
  ## credentialed (plan_review on) → cross-model critique + author
  ## revision; otherwise the plan reaches the campaign unreviewed, with a
  ## provenance stamp that says so.
  plan -> plan_review_topology
  plan_review_topology -> plan_review when do_review with {
    plan:        "{{outputs.plan.plan}}",
    assumptions: "{{outputs.plan.assumptions}}",
    risks:       "{{outputs.plan.risks}}"
  }
  plan_review_topology -> campaign when not do_review with {
    fail_log: "", plan: "{{outputs.plan.plan}}",
    plan_critique: "", plan_responses: "",
    plan_provenance: "authored by the plan node - NOT peer-reviewed (plan_review resolved off: one model family credentialed, or the operator said off); the author's own map, unchallenged"
  }
  plan_review -> plan_gate
  ## Peer served → the plan AUTHOR (same session) challenges the
  ## critique and integrates what holds; skipped (policy skip + peer
  ## unavailable) → the unreviewed plan proceeds, loudly.
  plan_gate -> plan_revise when not skipped with {
    plan:        "{{outputs.plan.plan}}",
    assumptions: "{{outputs.plan.assumptions}}",
    risks:       "{{outputs.plan.risks}}",
    concerns:    "{{outputs.plan_review.concerns}}",
    suggestions: "{{outputs.plan_review.suggestions}}",
    blocking:    "{{outputs.plan_review.blocking}}",
    _session_id: "{{outputs.plan._session_id}}",
    _session_fingerprint: "{{outputs.plan._session_fingerprint}}"
  }
  plan_gate -> campaign when skipped with {
    fail_log: "", plan: "{{outputs.plan.plan}}",
    plan_critique: "", plan_responses: "",
    plan_provenance: "authored by the plan node - the cross-model peer review was SKIPPED mid-run (peer unavailable, plan_review_policy skip); the author's own map, unchallenged"
  }
  plan_revise -> campaign with {
    fail_log:        "",
    plan:            "{{outputs.plan_revise.plan}}",
    plan_critique:   "{{outputs.plan_review.concerns}}",
    plan_responses:  "{{outputs.plan_revise.review_responses}}",
    plan_provenance: "authored by the plan node, critiqued by a cross-model peer, and revised by the author (its accepted/rejected responses follow)"
  }

  ## Each pass: the campaign ships feature slices (committing each in
  ## stride), then the deterministic build/test gate re-checks the tree.
  campaign -> verify_probe with {
    iteration: "{{loop.continuation_loop.iteration}}"
  }
  ## verify_probe reuses an already-valid verify.sh (skipping the LLM
  ## verify_build) on passes 2+; the first pass of every run regenerates it for
  ## the current tree. Both outgoing edges test the bool `fresh` (C012-exhaustive).
  verify_probe -> verify_run when fresh
  verify_probe -> verify_build when not fresh
  verify_build -> verify_run
  ## After the deterministic build gate, the adversarial review inspects the
  ## run's diff (the refinement pass moved in-loop), then the gate decides.
  verify_run -> review with {
    base_ref:       "{{vars.mr_base}}",
    build_passed:   "{{outputs.verify_run.passed}}",
    prior_findings: "{{outputs.gate.fail_log}}"
  }
  review -> gate

  ## Converged (green AND the campaign reports the feature fully shipped) →
  ## ship what is banked (optional PR, else finish).
  gate -> mr_gate when converged
  ## Not converged → another campaign pass. RED carries the failure log so the
  ## agent fixes what it broke; green-but-more-work carries an empty log so the
  ## agent simply continues the feature. This closes the single declared loop
  ## (campaign→verify_build→verify_run→gate→campaign); the loop-back reads
  ## outputs.gate.fail_log (a BODY node — never the loop-head campaign's own
  ## output on its back-edge, which would read a loop-entry-frozen value).
  ## The back-edge BLANKS the plan-phase fields: forward-edge mappings
  ## re-apply on every re-entry (outputs persist), and a stale pass-1
  ## plan would re-anchor later passes that must read `git log` instead.
  gate -> campaign as continuation_loop("{{vars.max_passes}}") with {
    fail_log: "{{outputs.gate.fail_log}}",
    plan: "", plan_critique: "", plan_responses: "", plan_provenance: ""
  }
  ## continuation_loop exhausted (max_passes hit while work remains) → ship
  ## what is banked anyway (every committed slice is real, landed work).
  ## Unconditional fallthrough after the loop-back retries are spent.
  gate -> mr_gate

  ## Converged (or capped) → optional PR path, else finish. finalize_mr pushes
  ## the run's HEAD (the series of per-slice commits) + opens the PR and
  ## (when source_issue_ref is set) posts the URL back. The feature prompt
  ## rides along as the PR's scope context.
  mr_gate -> forge_auth_probe when open_mr
  mr_gate -> done when not open_mr

  ## Push credential present → finalize_mr pushes + opens the PR; absent →
  ## finish cleanly (commits are on the engine's iterion/run/<name> storage
  ## branch for a manual push) instead of paying an agent turn to rediscover it.
  forge_auth_probe -> finalize_mr when available with {
    workspace_dir:    "{{vars.workspace_dir}}",
    mr_branch:        "{{vars.mr_branch}}",
    mr_base:          "{{vars.mr_base}}",
    source_issue_ref: "{{vars.source_issue_ref}}",
    scope_notes:      "{{vars.feature_prompt}}"
  }
  forge_auth_probe -> done when not available
  ## PR opened → surface its URL as a headline result-link in the run summary
  ## (deterministic stdout directive → preview_url_available kind=pr), then
  ## finish. Nothing opened (empty diff / refused PR) → straight to done.
  finalize_mr -> surface_pr_link when opened with { url: "{{outputs.finalize_mr.url}}" }
  finalize_mr -> done when not opened
  surface_pr_link -> done
